          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "hǎilàng — \"sea wave; a wave of the sea\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "hǎilàng tài dà le chuán kāi bu dào nàge dǎo — The waves are too big: the boat can't get to the island."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Its characters come later; for now it is a word for the ear."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "hǎilàng is built from 海 'the sea' and làng 'a wave', both inherited from Middle Chinese, xojX and langH (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription), which glosses the second 'waves'."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for getting around."
