          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "bèiké is built from bèi 'a shellfish' and ké 'a hard shell'. The first is inherited from Middle Chinese pajH (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription), glossed as a creature of the sea in a shell; the second is in the Guangyun only under an older spelling, read khaewk and glossed 'a hard skin', and the everyday ké does not follow from that reading, so its history is not settled."
            }
